<!--
    passthrough on SysV x86-64: a stated XMM parameter the wrapper does not
    get must not cost it the general-register parameters after it. The ARM
    hard-float side is kuna-passthrough-filegap-arm.xml.

    GNU as 2.38 (Ubuntu 22.04), linked with
    `ld -Ttext=0x401000 -e bits` without a build id:

        bits:      movq %xmm0,%rax; shr $52,%rax; mov %eax,(%rdi);
                   movq %xmm0,%rax; ret
        bits_tail: jmp bits
        sst:       movq %xmm0,(%rdi); mov (%rdi),%rax; shr $52,%rax; ret
        sst_tail:  jmp sst

    `bits` is `int bits(double x, int *p)` reading x's bits, the way gcc -O2
    moves a double it only takes apart; `sst` stores x through p and reads the
    word back. Each `_tail` forwards both arguments untouched.

    The single-function drive states each tail callee's recovered prototype
    first (option protoorder): `bits` as `(unsigned long a0 /* xmm0 */,
    unsigned int *a1 /* rdi */)`, and passthrough does not forward an XMM
    register stated as an integer. The call's argument list was extended in
    the stated order up to the first register it did not get, xmm0, so
    `bits_tail` printed `bits_tail(void)` with `bits();` and lost rdi. `sst`
    only stores xmm0 and states no arity claim, and the stored xmm0 refused
    the rdi its body reads, so `sst_tail` printed `sst_tail(void)`.

    SysV allocates XMM and general argument registers independently, so a gap
    in the XMM registers leaves rdi where it is: both wrappers now take rdi
    and pass it on. On Windows x64, which groups xmm0 with rcx, the same gap
    would move the pointer, and the extension still stops there.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring" (passthrough's
    extend_pending and stated_width). A strict correctness fix with no
    option: one pass pins the fixed output.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x401000" readonly="true">
66480f7ec048c1e834890766480f7ec0c3ebed660fd607488b0748c1e834c3eb
f2
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401000" name="bits"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401011" name="bits_tail"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401013" name="sst"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x40101f" name="sst_tail"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
